## Reproducibility

What to expect when re-running the experiments, and why some numbers can differ from ours.

### Timings

All times depend on the machine, and on its load. Run the experiments on an otherwise idle machine,
one at a time: the scripts are sequential for that reason, and two Ultimate or PaSTTeL runs sharing
the cores would slow each other down. Programs close to a timeout may be solved on a fast machine
and not on a slow one. Compare verdict counts, and totals and medians of the times, rather than
individual times.

### The lassos Ultimate extracts

On most programs Ultimate is deterministic: run twice with the same release, settings and Z3, it
analyses the same sequence of lassos and reports the same verdict, iterations and ranking
functions. On some long-running programs it is not. We ran ULR (`tools/UAutomizer-PaSTTeL-linux`,
LassoRanker backend) three times on 8 programs taken at random among the paper's programs with 4 to
12 lassos:

| Programs | Outcome of the 3 runs |
|----------|-----------------------|
| 7 of 8 | identical lasso sequences, verdicts, iterations and ranking functions -- including 3 programs whose three runs hit the 180 s timeout on the same lasso |
| `Ackermann_true-termination.c` | the runs pick different lassos from the 12th or the 16th lasso on, and all three hit the timeout |

The code of Ultimate and LassoRanker uses no unseeded randomness (the only random generator has a
fixed seed), and no solver query returned `unknown` before the divergence. When the runs part, the
abstraction automaton still has the same size in all of them: the same automaton yields a different
lasso. The likely cause is the order in which Ultimate iterates over hash-based collections of
objects hashed by identity, since the JVM assigns identity hash codes differently from one run to
the next. With sequential identity hash codes (`-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:hashCode=3`),
the two runs that started from the same hash counter produced the same 29 lassos, and only the run
whose counter was off by one diverged. This cannot be pinned down further from outside: Eclipse,
OSGi and the C parser start multithreaded and consume a varying number of hash codes before the
analysis begins. No Ultimate setting controls it.

What this means for each comparison:

- **ULR vs P-ULR** (per lasso trace): each comparison stays exact, since ULR-Baseline and P-ULR are
  measured on the very same dumped trace. What can vary on long-running programs is the *set* of
  traces extracted, hence the number of rows.
- **ULR vs UPL** (per program): the two sides are independent Ultimate runs, so on a long-running
  program they may explore different lassos. `--repeat` reports the median of several runs.

PaSTTeL's parallel configuration (`P-ULR-Par7`) races its techniques, and the first one to
conclude wins: which technique is reported, and its time, can differ between runs.

---

## CSV Output Format

### Per-lasso-trace CSV (`run_full_evaluation.sh`, `run_smoke_test.sh`)

The results CSV contains the following columns:

| Column                  | Description                                                                    |
|-------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `Trace Name`            | Path to the lasso trace file                                                   |
| `Result Code`           | Ultimate LassoRanker verdict: TERMINATING, NONTERMINATING, INFEASIBLE, UNKNOWN |
| `Fixpoint (ms)`         | Time for fixpoint computation in ULR                                           |
| `Termination (ms)`      | Time for termination analysis in ULR                                           |
| `Nontermination (ms)`   | Time for non-termination analysis in ULR                                       |
| `ULR-Baseline (ms)`     | Total execution time of ULR                                                    |
| `P-ULR-X`               | Total P-ULR-X execution time (X: Seq, Par2, Par4,...)                          |
| `PaSTTeL Status`        | PaSTTeL verdict: TERMINATING, NONTERMINATING, TIMEOUT, or NOT SUPPORTED        |
| `Stem Size`             | Number of stem transitions                                                     |
| `Loop Size`             | Number of loop transitions                                                     |
| `Total Size Trace`      | Total transitions                                                              |
| `Algo`                  | Ranking function template used by ULR-Baseline / P-ULR                         |

### Per-program CSV (`run_ulr_vs_upl.sh`)

`results_ULR_vs_UPL.csv` has one row per **program**, not per lasso trace:

| Column | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `Program`, `Ext` | Program file name and extension (`c` or `bpl`) |
| `Timeout (s)` | Budget given to each Ultimate run |
| `ULR Verdict`, `UPL Verdict` | `TERMINATING`, `NONTERMINATING`, `UNKNOWN`, `TIMEOUT`, `ERROR`, or `NO LOG` when that side was skipped |
| `ULR/UPL Wall (ms)` | Wall clock measured around the Ultimate process by the script |
| `ULR/UPL Plugin (ms)` | Time attributed to the BuchiAutomizer plugin, from Ultimate's own statistics |
| `ULR/UPL Lassos (ms)` | Time spent analysing lassos, from the same statistics |
| `ULR/UPL Iters` | Number of CEGAR iterations |
| `UPL PaSTTeL Calls` | How many times the PaSTTeL backend was consulted |
| `UPL PaSTTeL Success` | How many of those calls concluded |
| `UPL No Result` | How many produced no ranking function (UNKNOWN, timeout, crash or unmapped certificate) |
| `UPL Unmapped` | Certificates PaSTTeL produced that could not be mapped back |
| `UPL Techniques` | Winning PaSTTeL techniques, separated by `\|` |
| `Agreement` | both solved: `SAME` or `CONFLICT` (opposite verdicts); one solved: `UPL ONLY` or `ULR ONLY`; none solved: `BOTH <verdict>` (same failure) or `NEITHER` (different failures); `ULR/UPL MISSING` if a side was not run |
| `Speedup (wall)`, `Speedup (plugin)` | ULR time divided by UPL time, above 1 when UPL is faster; `-` unless both runs solved the program |


